/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A shock wave is a single pressure pulse, not a continuous sound.
   - Two effects break the stone.
   - Cavitation helps.
   - The classic source is electrohydraulic.
   - On an ellipsoid, the distance from focus F1 to any point on the surface plus the distance from that point to focus F2 is constant.
   - P is any point on the ellipsoidal reflector; F1 is the spark-gap focus; F2 is the focus where the stone sits; 2a is the constant path length, in metres.
   - An electromagnetic source fires a pulse of current through a flat coil behind a metal membrane.
   - Coupling matters more than any other detail.
   - Rate matters.
   - ESWL fails on very hard stones such as cystine, on stones above about 2 cm where the fragment load is too heavy, in obese patients where the skin-to-stone distance is long, and on stones low in the ureter where pelvic bone blocks the path.
*/
