/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Ice does not form inside the cell first.
   - Thaw is the second half of the kill.
   - Q is the heat removed, in joules (J); m is the mass of tissue, in kg; c is the specific heat of unfrozen tissue, about 3.5 kJ/kg·K; ΔT₁ is the fall from body temperature to freezing, in K; mw is the mass of water that freezes, in kg; L is t
   - A single freeze is unreliable.
   - Below about −40 °C almost no cell survives.
   - There are two ways to make a cold tip.
   - Liquid nitrogen boils at −196 °C at one atmosphere.
   - The Joule–Thomson effect is the cooling that happens when a compressed gas is forced through a narrow orifice and expands.
   - A thermocouple at the tip measures temperature.
   - Skin.
*/
