/* book exam traps: Cryotherapy removes heat; diathermy adds heat. They are opposite treatments with similar-sounding names. | A −196 °C tip does not mean −196 °C in the tissue. The ice front must pass −40 °C at the far edge of the lesion, so probe placement and imaging decide success. | One long freeze is weaker than two shorter freezes with a slow thaw between them. A quick thaw with no second freeze under-kills and the lesion returns. | Unit slips: latent heat is 334 kJ/kg = 334 000 J/kg, not 334 J/kg, and specific heat is per kg, not per gram. A stray factor of 1000 is the commonest arithmetic error here. | Oxygen deficiency has no warning smell. Nitrogen gas is colourless and odourless, so a leaking dewar in a small room causes dizziness and collapse before anyone notices. */
