/* book exam traps: Saying the protection circuit protects the patient. It protects the ECG amplifier; the patient is protected by paddle placement and by lead design. | Calling the neon lamp an indicator. In this circuit it is the shunt that fires at roughly 60–100 V and carries the shock current. | Mixing the two voltage levels: an ablation tip runs at about 70 V, a defibrillator at up to 5000 V. Both can meet the same electrode lead. | Writing that ablation kills tissue with a shock. It heats tissue with continuous RF current at about 500 kHz, 20–50 W, holding the tip at 60–70 °C. | Forgetting recovery time. A protected amplifier is still saturated for a moment after the shock, so the first part of the trace is unusable. */
